What was derived
----------------

* **The declarative tool-recipe pattern.** CyberStrikeAI describes each
  assessment tool in a YAML file — the binary, its typed parameters, how each
  parameter maps onto a flag or position, and prose describing when to use it.
  swarmstrike's `swarmstrike/tools/recipes.py` and the files under
  `swarmstrike/tools/recipes/` follow that design.

* **The tool coverage.** The set of tools described under
  `swarmstrike/tools/recipes/` was selected from CyberStrikeAI's `tools/`
  directory. Recipes derived this way carry a comment saying so.

* **The agent and role taxonomy.** The division of an engagement into recon,
  vulnerability triage, privilege escalation, lateral movement, persistence,
  exfiltration and reporting follows the agent definitions under
  CyberStrikeAI's `agents/`.

* **The screen layout of the web console.** The set of views the dashboard
  offers — campaign overview, findings, agents, tool inventory — follows
  CyberStrikeAI's web console.

What was changed
----------------

Per section 4(b) of the licence, the following changes were made to the
derived material:

* **Language and runtime.** CyberStrikeAI is written in Go and orchestrates
  agents with Eino. swarmstrike is written in Python and orchestrates agents
  with the Claude Agent SDK. No Go source was copied; the recipes were
  rewritten and the descriptions translated and reworded.

* **A risk tier on every recipe, gated by a signed contract.** Each recipe
  declares how far it goes — passive, active, intrusive, credentialed,
  privilege escalation, post-exploitation, lateral movement, persistence,
  exfiltration, evasion or destructive — and that tier is checked against the
  engagement contract's rules of engagement before the command is built.
  CyberStrikeAI governs tool use through role-scoped tool policies and
  human-in-the-loop approval; swarmstrike adds a machine-checked engagement
  scope on top, so a command against an unauthorized target is refused whether
  or not an operator is paying attention.

* **Parameters become an argument vector, never a command string.** Values are
  typed, pattern-checked, and rejected if they contain shell syntax, so a
  parameter cannot introduce a second command.

* **No general-purpose shell tool.** CyberStrikeAI's `exec`,
  `execute-python-script` and `install-python-package` recipes were not
  ported as such. swarmstrike replaces them with three tiers: a structured
  command (allowlisted binary plus argument vector, fully scope-checked), a
  pipeline (stages piped between containers, with later stages restricted to
  filters that cannot execute), and a shell or script tier that requires a
  per-invocation human approval which cannot be configured off.

* **Payload generation and implant building were not ported.** CyberStrikeAI's
  `msfvenom` recipe and its `internal/c2` payload and beacon builders have no
  counterpart here. swarmstrike's red team tiers orchestrate established
  external frameworks rather than producing new implants.

* **Destructive actions are refused unconditionally.** No contract setting
  enables them.
